WEBVTT

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:14.730
 I mean, I already had these political ideas that even, you know, predated my entrance into the food system studies, but I didn't really get a sense of what it was like for the people who were actually doing that work.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:23.360
 I never made a movie before in my life. I just thought, oh, this will be a great vehicle to talk about these issues and to create media about it, but then also for myself to learn.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:32.900
 It was sort of a research project, and one of my thoughts was, I'm not going to get a lot of small farmers to talk to me unless I say it's for a movie.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:44.320
 It was pretty much a way of getting people to talk to me, and my questions for them were largely about, you know, what is a sustainable food system, what defines it to you, and what is preventing it from existing?

00:00:44.320 --> 00:01:02.780
 You know, so much of the narrative at that point, the 2007, 2008, was really about the voting with your fork and this notion of, you know, if we just support these small-scale family farmers, organic farmers, whatever you want to call them, that that's the best way we can ensure that they expand and flourish.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:16.340
 But then talking to a lot of these farmers, even if they were doing okay, you come up against the reality that it's actually a systemic or a structural constraint, not an individual one and not one that's caused by a lack of purchase interest.

