 Check it out at anti-dogmatist.com.

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:42.650
 Antonio Roman Alcala is a food activist, gardener, teacher, and scholar.

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:49.700
 With a group of friends, he broke into a vacant lot by the freeway in the southern part of San Francisco to start Alamany Farm.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:54.680
 He has taught ecological horticulture there and at many other food projects.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:00.560
 He managed a food justice project and garden at San Francisco's Potrero Hill Public Housing

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.160
 and organized the San Francisco Urban Agriculture Alliance.

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:11.020
 He made a movie called In Search of Good Food and worked on forming the California Food Policy Council.

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:13.010
 He was part of Occupy the Farm.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:19.030
 He recently got a master's degree at the Institute for Social Studies at The Hague for research on food sovereignty.

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:24.030
 He's also a musician and new father, and he lives in San Francisco with his family.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:25.530
 Hey, Antonio.

00:03:25.770 --> 00:03:26.610
 Welcome to the show.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:27.190
 Hello.

00:03:27.190 --> 00:03:36.070
 So, I guess I wanted to start today talking about your roots in, like, DIY culture and, you know,

00:03:36.070 --> 00:03:43.100
 the beginning of Alamany Farm and maybe how you got started in working with food.

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:43.520
 Yeah.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:44.080
 What that meant.

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:50.310
 So, I definitely, I would say it's accurate to portray it as coming out of the DIY culture

00:03:50.310 --> 00:04:00.620
 because I was first a musician and a high school dropout and sort of someone who was interested in pursuing life on my own terms

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:06.770
 rather than for achieving some sort of career goals or things like that.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:13.450
 And so, a lot of the projects I was involved with at the time that I got into food were really about creating my own media,

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:18.350
 so doing zines, about, you know, creating alternative sort of street culture.

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:24.090
 There was things that was very much about doing it yourself or, as I say, D.I.O., doing it ourselves

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:26.370
 because a lot of it was also collaborative projects.

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:32.200
 So, my entrance into food was really because I was interested in politics.

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:37.380
 A lot of the bands I was in, a lot of the projects I was involved in were political in nature.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:42.700
 So, they might have been about anti-war organizing or here in San Francisco, a lot of anti-gentrification organizing.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:48.760
 And food came up as a thing that, you know, it's sort of become a cliche at this point,

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:54.020
 but, you know, everyone eats and it's this way that you can engage people in this very tangible way

00:04:54.020 --> 00:04:59.880
 of taking control of some aspect of our lives, which is usually considered sort of outside of our control.

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:07.350
 And so, I was a part of a group of people, young people, who were idealistically interested in starting intentional communities,

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:10.730
 even though most of us didn't have very much money to start such communities

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:14.610
 and we didn't necessarily have a lot of practice in working in intentional communities.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:21.140
 But we had this ideal of, oh, what if we could start an urban intentional community that was connected to a rural one

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:26.460
 and have them sort of interpenetrating instead of it being this thing of, oh, going back to the land

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:31.780
 or being fully urbanized, that we could talk about the connections between urban and rural

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:40.240
 and use food as the sort of foundation for discussing politics of sustainability and of, you know, equitable systems.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:47.610
 And so, that was the project that caused us to seek somewhere to practice our collectivity.

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:51.670
 And so, we found, my mom lives up the hill from Alamany Farm.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:56.790
 And so, we knew of it, I knew of it as a site and it had basically been abandoned.

00:05:57.510 --> 00:06:01.370
 And so, we thought maybe this would be a good place to start off.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:04.270
 And that was how I started that project.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:11.730
 I had already at that time, that was 2005, February of 2005 was when we started actually actively farming there

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:15.630
 and actively working in the community to sort of decide what the project should look like.

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:23.310
 I had already been doing some gardening for a couple years, a year or two before then.

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:27.850
 So, I had been going to this place called the All-in-Common Garden on 23rd Street.

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:33.790
 I had a couple of mentors who were very much more sort of hippie or anarchist farmer types.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:36.670
 And they were teaching me more the tangible skills.

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:39.710
 And I actually, I should step back.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:46.010
 One thing that was funny is I got into the food stuff partly because I was sort of in an apocalyptic mindset.

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:51.370
 And I thought, oh my God, peak oil and all this stuff is happening and we're all going to, you know,

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:52.950
 the system is going to come crashing down.

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:58.070
 So, I should know how, first thought was I should know how to identify wild foods that I could eat at any point.

00:06:58.170 --> 00:07:00.170
 So, I started studying all the weeds and things.

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:03.590
 And then I realized, oh, well, growing food might be a good idea as well.

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:06.930
 And so, that was the step that brought me into these gardening projects.

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:11.890
 And then Alamany Farm was the first place where it really became something that I was doing as a practice

00:07:11.890 --> 00:07:14.110
 and really, really learning quickly by doing.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:20.090
 And then also trying to figure out a way to best share that information, that knowledge with other people

00:07:20.090 --> 00:07:23.590
 and to have it be a project that was really open to a lot of people to participate.

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:34.180
 So, that was, yeah, I think that was my first entrance into it was, hey, how can I use food to discuss these larger political issues?

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:45.740
 And then how can I just sort of create personal resilience by understanding how to grow food myself and to create more community that is capable of doing that in case, you know, things go bad or whatever.

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:51.360
 At this point, I'm less, I have less affinity for the apocalyptic sort of position.

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:54.820
 But it was definitely a motivating force initially.

00:07:56.420 --> 00:08:07.840
 Well, I think we should delve into that apocalyptic thing a little bit more because I think that space between hope and despair is really a thread that runs through, like, a lot of the work that you've done.

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:10.320
 So, you started working with Alamany Farm.

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:13.920
 And then after that, like, when did the film project start?

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:25.440
 Yeah, it was, the film was definitely in a large sense my next step because, so I thought, okay, people are urban, but we need to gain more control over our food or understand it better.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.140
 Let's start this urban food project.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:33.320
 And then I realized there was a limitation to that in terms of understanding the, quote, unquote, food system.

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:41.070
 And so, the movie, I started the project in 2007 when I started researching and figuring out who I could interview.

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:45.070
 And I worked with the California, it was originally called the California Food and Justice Coalition.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:46.850
 Now, it's the Community Food Justice Coalition.

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:56.840
 I was working with them to figure out, okay, well, could I do, like, a tour around California and interview people who have to do with many different parts of the food system?

00:08:57.020 --> 00:09:08.860
 And not just urban farms, but actually rural farms and also farm worker organizations and also groups working on indigenous fisheries and all this different stuff that was going on around food.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:15.560
 And I had this overly ambitious idea that I was going to make eight different movies about eight different themes in the food system.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:17.780
 And I'd never made a movie before in my life.

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:23.590
 I just thought, oh, this will be a great vehicle to talk about these issues and to create media about it.

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:25.560
 But then also for myself to learn.

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:27.060
 It was sort of a research project.

00:09:28.700 --> 00:09:36.440
 And one of my thoughts was, I'm not going to get a lot of small farmers to talk to me unless I say it's for a movie.

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:39.420
 It was pretty much a way of getting people to talk to me.

00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:46.950
 And so, yeah, that was a really enlightening experience because it really forced me to think.

00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:53.130
 I mean, I already had these political ideas that even, you know, predated my entrance into the food system studies.

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:59.120
 But but I didn't really get a sense of what it was like for the people who were actually doing that work.

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:04.300
 And my questions for them were largely about, you know, what is a sustainable food system?

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:07.340
 What defines it to you and what is preventing it from existing?

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:20.480
 And I think that that was that was the story I tried to tell was about how, you know, so much of the narrative at that point, the 2007, 2008 was really about the buying voting with your fork.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:31.500
 And this notion of, you know, if we just support these small scale family farmers, organic farmers, whatever you want to call them, that that's the best way we can ensure that they expand and flourish.

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:44.940
 But then talking to a lot of these farmers, even if they were doing OK, you come up against the reality that it's actually a systemic or a structural constraint, not not an individual one and not one that's caused by a lack of purchase interest.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:51.000
 So that was the story that that movie ended up telling to me.

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:55.100
 And then that's what I tried to present in the final version of it.

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:56.860
 I just watched it last night.

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:01.620
 I've seen bits of it before, but I was just really struck thinking about that.

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:12.180
 That moment for myself and like what a hopeful time 2007 and 2008 felt for me like it felt like there was all this room for imagining that there was this real sweetness in the food movement.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:16.970
 But I'm kind of curious, like, what's it like to be part of a conversation over time?

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:19.190
 Where is that conversation at now?

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:25.590
 That's a that's a good question, because I feel like the vote with your fork narrative has not gone away.

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:26.430
 That's for sure.

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:35.470
 But at the same time, I think you see things like was his name, Josh Fertel, who was at some point the head of Slow Food USA.

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:40.390
 So Slow Food is known to be all about, you know, it's sort of this elitist thing.

00:11:40.390 --> 00:11:44.430
 And it's really about buying the best foods and really enjoying them.

00:11:44.510 --> 00:11:52.090
 And it seems it's always had this image of being very distant from this question of structural power and changing things at a fundamental basis.

00:11:52.090 --> 00:11:55.870
 But Josh Fertel himself actually said that line at some point.

00:11:55.950 --> 00:11:58.590
 He said it's not enough to talk about voting with your fork.

00:11:58.650 --> 00:12:03.610
 We need to vote with our votes and we need to vote with our bodies and we need to vote with, you know, as collectivities.

00:12:03.610 --> 00:12:05.650
 We need to think beyond the individual.

00:12:06.430 --> 00:12:15.050
 So I think that there's a certain extent to which that narrative has now hit a sort of more mainstream within the food movement.

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:34.110
 But it's also a challenge because, you know, in this culture, things become sort of fetishized or just a lot of times things that are what I call countercultural get absorbed into the dominant culture or get sort of mishmashed into becoming a subculture.

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:40.930
 It's not necessarily counter to the fundamental problems of the mainstream culture.

00:12:40.930 --> 00:12:52.180
 They just become a thing that you can adopt as part of your own individual sort of experience and culture that represent to you an alternative, but don't necessarily embody that counterness.

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:57.880
 And that's what I think has happened a lot with food movement, for example, food justice.

00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:13.840
 I feel like when I was starting Alamany Farm with people, the peer base that I was working with, a lot of people were really rooted in very radical politics, very anti-capitalist, explicitly anti-capitalist politics, very race aware.

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:17.700
 You know, a lot of it was about the racial aspects of the food system.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:20.680
 And to me, that's what food justice work was about.

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:28.040
 It was about fundamentally saying that it wasn't OK for people to not have access to food.

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:29.300
 And it's not just about charity.

00:13:29.400 --> 00:13:40.200
 It's about people reclaiming power over the sources of food, whether it's very directly growing food, but also about policies, also about, you know, ownership of land and housing, etc.

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:44.230
 So to me, I thought that that food justice was very radical.

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:52.990
 And now I feel like there's been a flourishing of food justice, quote unquote, projects, but many of them might be ultimately just about the food access issue.

00:13:52.990 --> 00:14:03.470
 Some of them really consider themselves food justice because their issue is we want to make sure that organic foods get to poor people or, you know, communities of color or something like that.

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:21.050
 And I think that it's not to disparage those projects, but there's that process by which things that are not OK with the way things are become just a way to tweak at the margins and just a way to sort of excuse.

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:29.690
 It's not intended to excuse the system as it is, but it's really more about like, oh, we know it's problematic, but our solution doesn't really address those issues.

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:32.750
 It's really just mitigating the problems.

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:37.260
 Well, and let's talk about that in terms of, I guess, your trajectory.

00:14:37.560 --> 00:14:37.880
 Right.

00:14:37.960 --> 00:14:46.140
 So you started with this kind of DIY approach and a pretty interesting one because you are a San Francisco native.

00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:49.660
 And this was this place that was right by your mom's house.

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:51.800
 And, you know, you knew people in the neighborhood.

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:55.100
 It was like it was a deeply radical in many ways.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:57.130
 And it worked.

00:14:57.350 --> 00:14:57.550
 Right.

00:14:57.590 --> 00:15:00.850
 And then all of a sudden it was cool and there was a tension.

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:01.880
 And that was weird.

00:15:02.080 --> 00:15:02.260
 Yeah.

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:02.540
 Right.

00:15:02.620 --> 00:15:04.500
 Like and it was there was all this hope.

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:13.330
 And then it was like the conversation got a lot deeper for everybody or people's lives went on and they like went on to become doctors and lawyers and whatever people do.

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:19.010
 But for you, it's like meant that you're like spend a lot of time in institutional space.

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:19.450
 Right.

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:23.730
 Like by working with policy or by doing these other things.

00:15:23.730 --> 00:15:26.190
 Like what does that mean?

00:15:26.190 --> 00:15:35.500
 Like to keep bringing radical politics to that or these values like in places where people could say, oh, maybe he sold out or something like that.

00:15:35.500 --> 00:15:43.490
 Well, that's I definitely think it's it's a line and and it's hard to advocate.

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:49.820
 It's kind of like it's hard to advocate the right way of going about making change.

00:15:49.900 --> 00:16:03.020
 I'm a firm believer that people have either implicit or explicit theories of change about what they should be doing in order to make change and that those theories of change often conflict between people.

00:16:03.200 --> 00:16:07.500
 And they can actually conflict within the person or they can change over time.

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:21.710
 And I think that there's no set standard that you can really apply for one person because it's so sort of ideological or just based in.

00:16:21.930 --> 00:16:37.260
 I mean, I like to think my theory of change is informed by my studies, for example, but I would never presume that because someone else has a different one that theirs is just automatically incorrect because of my experience and my background.

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:50.580
 So I guess what I'm saying is in the in the realm of something like selling out, I think there are clear examples where people do sell out or lose their sort of fundamental values that they held at some point in the past.

00:16:51.460 --> 00:17:06.260
 But I also think there's a process of transition where people become incorporated into institutions or incorporated into cultures where they cannot act a certain way without losing the leverage point that they've achieved.

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:13.600
 And I don't think we should say, oh, that person's work is not valuable anymore because they've, quote unquote, sold out.

00:17:13.600 --> 00:17:15.780
 I think it's actually a case by case basis.

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:20.140
 And sometimes sometimes there needs to be people called out or whatever.

00:17:20.140 --> 00:17:25.440
 And sometimes it's really more just about accepting that there are many means towards change.

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:41.500
 So for me, my thing is, how can we retain a radical analysis, meaning getting to the root, meaning acknowledging these these are structural issues rather than individual issues, not just accepting the frames and the narratives that are mainstream culture puts on all problems.

00:17:41.500 --> 00:17:56.500
 The silver bullet solutions, the silver bullet solutions, government is going to change everything or, you know, and how do we integrate that into all spaces and how do we maintain that analysis without necessarily that meaning a sort of all or nothing, nothing strategy?

00:17:56.560 --> 00:18:12.900
 Because so so so having worked with a lot of anti-capitalists, my main problem with that scene is that it often takes this notion of like, well, I've studied all this highfalutin political literature and it says that the state is the problem.

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:14.300
 The state is a capitalist state.

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:18.180
 The state just wants to improve, improve capitalists ability to make money.

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:26.880
 The state will never be a positive force for a fundamental transformation of society and dismantling white supremacy and patriarchy and all these things.

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:39.430
 And I can agree with that analysis without thinking that that means that you should never engage the state or never deal with policy or never deal with commerce as a potential means towards change.

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:47.040
 I just don't think that having a radical analysis means you dismiss any action that's short of complete revolution.

00:18:47.890 --> 00:18:49.810
 So that's my personal theory of change.

00:18:49.890 --> 00:19:03.720
 I believe that if we want to make a really functional food movement, we need to think in terms of these different approaches and how they can actually be synergistic rather than be, you know, tearing each other apart based on sort of ideological purity.

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:15.580
 And I think I want to be clear that goes both ways, because I think that I know people who work on policy who are incredibly dismissive of any critique of capitalism who say, oh, well, you're not going to change that.

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:18.160
 So why even bother having that analysis?

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:20.780
 We need to deal with the concrete policy.

00:19:20.900 --> 00:19:28.940
 And I think that's also BS because you're it's a cop out to say that the system is very difficult to change and we don't know exactly how to change it.

00:19:28.980 --> 00:19:31.960
 Therefore, we shouldn't think about it or address it or talk about it.

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:52.330
 You know, so my thing is that all people who maintain their personal theories of change that usually fall along this sort of, you know, revolutionary to reformist spectrum need to consider their own theories of change, like be self-critical about them and be able to actually engage with others who don't share their own theories of change.

00:19:52.330 --> 00:20:07.910
 And I think that's historically been the way that movements are most successful, because generally to change usually very recalcitrant institutions, you have to have the pressure of a very radical sort.

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:10.490
 Something that says, shit's going to go down.

00:20:10.630 --> 00:20:12.130
 We're going to change the whole system.

00:20:12.490 --> 00:20:19.150
 And then you need the people inside the system in various leverage points of power that say, we don't want it to go that far.

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:23.410
 But if we do this or that reform, then it'll calm it down for a while.

00:20:23.870 --> 00:20:26.530
 And I think that that's how change generally happens.

00:20:26.530 --> 00:20:35.090
 And you need both the reformists who are sort of on the inside and the radicals pushing from the outside to make that happen more quickly, I think.

00:20:35.090 --> 00:20:56.110
 Well, as you're talking about this, and I'm thinking about the article you wrote about the Berkeley Food Institute, when you were involved in kind of the beginning imaginations of what that institution could be within UC Berkeley, that this common thread with your work is that you're deeply curious, right?

00:20:56.110 --> 00:21:12.570
 And I think that engaging with these bigger systemic things in this way that is actually authentically curious feels so interesting, because it's not explicitly hopeful or full of despair, but it kind of exists somewhere in between, right?

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:16.710
 Sometimes it's hopeful, sometimes it's not, but it gives people credit where it's due.

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:21.230
 So that seems like something that could last over time.

00:21:21.230 --> 00:21:28.780
 And to me, that seems like part of that is you having so many different hats that you wear.

00:21:29.620 --> 00:21:39.300
 So you look at your life kind of as, we were talking about this before we started recording, of all these different parts that make up a whole life.

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:44.280
 So can you describe maybe the story of that and what that means to you?

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:50.710
 Well, I just want to respond to your point, though, I think is, you know, my email is anti-dogmatist.

00:21:50.710 --> 00:22:01.140
 And I think that curiosity is really tied to that, that since I was a teen, I've had this idea that no matter what I believed in,

00:22:01.220 --> 00:22:06.560
 I need to make sure that what I believe isn't a dogma, that it's something I'm always curious about.

00:22:06.760 --> 00:22:10.520
 Maybe there's something wrong with what I believe, or maybe there's something not quite right about it.

00:22:10.580 --> 00:22:22.920
 And that's the thing about the working with institutions, that I do believe that, you know, generally states are dedicated to accumulation and generally corporations don't have our best interests.

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:32.080
 These are things that I believe, but I will never bring them all the way to the point of being a dogma where I don't allow for the possibility that something might be different.

00:22:32.460 --> 00:22:35.220
 Right. And so that's I think that's where that comes from.

00:22:35.560 --> 00:22:51.710
 And then as far as the the sort of the being, you know, diversity of of interests that really did come from similarly from the sort of DIY upbringing I had as well.

00:22:51.770 --> 00:22:53.670
 Well, first off, I went to an arts high school.

00:22:53.670 --> 00:23:01.640
 So in this arts high school, you know, everyone was bohemian and doing interesting, creative things and being weirdos.

00:23:02.060 --> 00:23:23.170
 And and and I think that started me on a path where I understood being a native San Franciscan, as well as someone who was part of that sort of very artistic community that you could sort of create your own personality or not personality, your own identity, your own ideas of what it what it was OK to be.

00:23:23.450 --> 00:23:45.740
 And and and so because I dropped out of high school because I was involved in these punk rock scenes that were really about rejecting the dominant narratives of society, I figured that I'd never wanted to be someone who had a career like I never wanted to be someone whose main form of identity was based on what I did to make money.

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:57.210
 And so I really just focused on pursuing the things I actually cared about and in a sort of, you know, hopeful way, hoping that I would be provided for.

00:23:57.310 --> 00:24:04.430
 And there's a certain level of privilege that enabled that, you know, for example, I mean, I left my mom's house when I was 17.

00:24:04.710 --> 00:24:22.520
 But the fact that my mom owned a house or was buying a house in San Francisco rather than renting that bumped our class status up from solidly working class from my whole childhood to like, OK, now I feel like if all else fails and I don't do well in my life, I can at least move back in with my mom.

00:24:22.520 --> 00:24:31.860
 You know, so there's a certain level of which privilege enabled me to have this approach where I was just, you know what, I'm going to I love playing music.

00:24:32.140 --> 00:24:36.280
 I love organizing, you know, communities against things we don't like.

00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:38.700
 I like, you know, making zines.

00:24:38.820 --> 00:24:39.600
 I like traveling.

00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:46.510
 I decided that these are the things that make me happy and they make me part of the world.

00:24:46.710 --> 00:24:49.990
 And so I didn't need to just do one as a career.

00:24:49.990 --> 00:25:00.050
 And so it was from age 16 that I dropped out of high school to 23 or so that I just pursued all these different interests.

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:08.270
 And that was when towards the tail end of that, I found the food thing as something that I was becoming more and more interested in.

00:25:08.410 --> 00:25:16.050
 And it just it actually it became a point where I realized you literally can't do everything at once because there's only so much time in the day.

00:25:16.050 --> 00:25:21.770
 So I was trying to be a comic book artist and a graffiti artist and a musician and a community organizer.

00:25:21.770 --> 00:25:23.750
 And I have, of course, I had to make some money.

00:25:23.850 --> 00:25:29.990
 So I was working whatever, you know, food service job two to three days a week, the bare minimum to be able to pay my rent.

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:33.290
 And then I would try to dumpster dive to, you know, get the rest of my food.

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:37.130
 And luckily at that point in San Francisco, there was still a lot.

00:25:37.130 --> 00:25:48.200
 I would say I had a really solid community in the sense that a lot of things that I would need to to survive, I could actually get through my community rather than through needing money.

00:25:49.260 --> 00:25:58.570
 So there was a lot of this, the circumstances that enabled me to focus on what I wanted to be doing rather than the need to earn.

00:25:58.810 --> 00:26:04.170
 And I just earned enough based on whatever job I could get just to sustain the other things.

00:26:04.550 --> 00:26:15.270
 And then that point at which I realized that the food and urban sustainability was my real passion for what I wanted to change.

00:26:15.490 --> 00:26:23.200
 And I mean, of course, I was interested in many other topics, but that was something that I came upon as, you know, I really want to get farther into this.

00:26:23.360 --> 00:26:36.040
 And so to the extent to which I have specialized in anything, it's only because I've discovered a particular enthusiasm within myself to sort of focus a little bit more.

00:26:36.040 --> 00:26:39.800
 And that's what my recent master's student master's program.

00:26:40.510 --> 00:26:46.150
 It was the first time that I've only done two things in my life rather than five different things.

00:26:46.630 --> 00:27:01.540
 And it was because I, A, needed some distance from San Francisco for many reasons, mostly because of the gentrification and the sort of not knowing whether I still belonged here in a way and wanted to get some distance and perspective from that.

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:14.940
 And then also because I was invited in this program, which was really exciting about linking my work, you know, in the global north and in the belly of the beast with the work of other people doing food and agricultural work from around the world.

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:26.270
 And, you know, the program is run by June Boris, who's a very respected activist scholar who combines work as an activist with work as a scholar, which is exactly what I'm interested in doing.

00:27:26.390 --> 00:27:31.460
 How can I actually be someone who doesn't just identify as one thing?

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:36.060
 And so I feel like it's worked for me.

00:27:36.160 --> 00:27:43.820
 Like I said, it's definitely based on some level of privilege, but I've seen other people do this as well, not from such positions of privilege.

00:27:43.820 --> 00:28:04.270
 I think it's really fundamentally about whether you can just sort of have this faith and decide to follow a path that makes sense to you rather than the notions of security or sort of specialization that our society usually puts on people, especially those who end up going to school.

00:28:04.270 --> 00:28:17.450
 I feel like, you know, for many people, they're specialized because they have to have a job to sustain their family and they simply look for the most the least offensive version out there that they can find.

00:28:17.450 --> 00:28:26.950
 And so going to school is one way to achieve that outcome as opposed to going to school to learn about a subject matter that you care about, you know.

00:28:27.190 --> 00:28:51.460
 And and I think that's why I encourage anybody to drop out of high school, you know, because because I feel like that was what enabled me to focus on on pursuing the things I wanted to learn about, which led me to a position where I happened to be old enough to be self-disciplined in school rather than being like, you know, rejecting all things authoritarian because that was my younger self.

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:57.540
 I was able to go to school at that time, which enabled me to get better grades, which enabled me to get these different scholarships.

00:28:57.780 --> 00:29:03.060
 So so it sort of led to the path where it enabled me to continue doing all these different things.

00:29:04.440 --> 00:29:11.890
 Let's yeah, let's focus back in on San Francisco because that I feel like that's a big character in your story.

00:29:11.890 --> 00:29:22.810
 Right. So in 2005, starting Alamany Farm, there there was this spirit of this DIY culture here that felt it was.

00:29:22.890 --> 00:29:33.850
 After the first dot com bubble had burst and you said, you know, that you had this community that felt like a lot of things that you could source a lot of things from your community.

00:29:33.850 --> 00:29:44.490
 Right. And I think remembering that time that that probably meant food and a place to stay and entertainment and health care sometimes.

00:29:44.490 --> 00:29:49.550
 And, you know, like, yeah, I didn't have health insurance most of the time.

00:29:49.550 --> 00:30:03.860
 But I think there was this moment where these projects started, not just Alamany, but other projects in this town, too, you know, that called themselves urban farms instead of gardens.

00:30:04.300 --> 00:30:11.910
 And I think maybe like what is an urban farm and why isn't it a garden?

00:30:11.910 --> 00:30:18.380
 And like, what is that establishing of space or that like occupying of something different?

00:30:19.940 --> 00:30:26.350
 Well, I mean, I definitely think there's a little bit of a only a semantic difference between a farm and a garden.

00:30:26.350 --> 00:30:42.480
 And I think that really it's it's sort of in some ways it's a narrative device to say that an urban farm exists is to call into question the notion that farming is something that only takes place in a rural landscape.

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:58.150
 That said, I think that what for at least in the case of Alamany Farm and definitely Hayes Valley Farm, that some of these projects and free farm, actually, the three main largest farms that existed, one only one of which still remains.

00:30:59.270 --> 00:31:03.090
 They were all premised on a collective labor model.

00:31:03.090 --> 00:31:08.450
 They were all about bringing people together to grow food together rather than individual plots.

00:31:09.050 --> 00:31:15.750
 And it wasn't a garden because these are places where your your goal is to grow food.

00:31:15.930 --> 00:31:22.350
 Right. A garden, theoretically, a garden sort of in your mind has a function of beauty.

00:31:22.630 --> 00:31:25.150
 It's really sort of a space to be in.

00:31:25.210 --> 00:31:27.970
 And of course, all of these sites played that role.

00:31:27.970 --> 00:31:41.370
 But they were all really focused on food production in various ways, which to me made them more of a farm because they were really about larger scale for the city production of food.

00:31:42.270 --> 00:31:49.070
 And I think that, you know, they're either San Francisco, you're right, plays a big role in my story.

00:31:49.070 --> 00:32:03.840
 But also, if you talk to anyone in San Francisco about urban agriculture who's been involved for a while, you see how much it tells San Francisco itself and its transitions and its connections much wider economically and culturally.

00:32:04.100 --> 00:32:18.500
 Tell the story of how no farming system operates in a vacuum, right, whether that's urban or rural, like all these systems take place in relationship to things that happen outside the farm and outside the community.

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:32.980
 And so in the case of San Francisco, what it tells me and I've actually been trying to slowly work on a academic article about this, which is that we might have all these ideals about how we want to set up these projects.

00:32:32.980 --> 00:32:37.360
 So, for example, Alamany Farm really started with this idea of horizontal organization.

00:32:37.540 --> 00:32:47.730
 We were an open group that operated on consensus and we, you know, tried to have participation really drive all of the decisions and how the project ran.

00:32:47.950 --> 00:32:50.830
 And it's still theoretically run like that to this day.

00:32:51.610 --> 00:32:57.620
 But who gets to participate, who can participate, is framed by the economics of the city itself.

00:32:58.100 --> 00:33:08.240
 And there's simply not the base of people who used to be involved at Alamany Farm now than there was 10 years ago because of the continual gentrification.

00:33:08.780 --> 00:33:17.240
 So even 2005 was already past the first dot-com wave, which did cause lots of displacement, which did cause an increase in the cost of living, you know.

00:33:17.540 --> 00:33:23.730
 And yet, even at that time, there was still this solid feeling of this community existed and was here.

00:33:23.730 --> 00:33:38.280
 And many, especially young people who came here because of the image of San Francisco and they wanted to participate in something that was about creating positive social change, those people just simply cannot even afford to live that sort of lifestyle in the city now.

00:33:38.720 --> 00:33:50.910
 And so what that told me is that you can never do activism purely on this sort of without an acknowledgement or an awareness of these other factors that happen.

00:33:51.290 --> 00:33:54.950
 We don't we can't necessarily fully control the forces of gentrification.

00:33:54.950 --> 00:33:58.110
 I think there's a whole other discussion to be had about what can be done.

00:33:58.510 --> 00:34:05.590
 But let's just say it's clear we can't, you know, stop it once it's happening in some sort of significant sense.

00:34:05.730 --> 00:34:09.570
 So then what does that mean for how we set up these urban projects?

00:34:09.590 --> 00:34:15.590
 Like, what are the benefits of them if they only have a certain lifespan because of gentrification?

00:34:16.030 --> 00:34:24.450
 And what does that mean for places like Oakland, which were which are on the next level, the next wave of gentrification from San Francisco?

00:34:24.690 --> 00:34:31.920
 And they're there. I mean, if you ask anybody, there's as much, if not more urban food projects focused on food justice.

00:34:32.420 --> 00:34:34.740
 I mean, there's all the history and legacy of the Black Panthers.

00:34:34.920 --> 00:34:36.360
 There's so much going on there.

00:34:36.460 --> 00:34:46.070
 But now if you were to start an urban farm there, that has very different implications and potentially very negative pro gentrification implications.

00:34:47.350 --> 00:35:06.020
 And so to me, the San Francisco story really brings up the importance of if you're going to be an urban food activist, being very attentive to those political economic sides of your project, rather than just assuming that, you know, when you grow organic food in a community, it's a positive thing.

00:35:06.290 --> 00:35:24.710
 Absolutely. And I think I talk about that time because of this idea of calling it a farm, I think, links it with farmers everywhere else in the world and positions people as something other than hobbyists.

00:35:25.230 --> 00:35:31.120
 And I think symbolizes this creation of an alternative system.

00:35:31.930 --> 00:35:34.170
 Right. And I think there's a lot to unpack in that.

00:35:34.230 --> 00:35:35.630
 There's a lot of problems with that.

00:35:35.630 --> 00:35:44.900
 But I think there is an impetus there that feels important, right, that feels like there is power to control your city in a different way.

00:35:45.520 --> 00:36:01.580
 Well, I mean, definitely, definitely, I would say that all of these projects are at least the large bulk of them that I've been involved with and that other people I know have been involved with are premised on the fact that our society is so de-peasantized that we have less than 1% farmers.

00:36:01.780 --> 00:36:09.540
 And most of those farmers are really more like land managers in their air-conditioned tractors, you know, large fields.

00:36:09.740 --> 00:36:19.790
 So part of the goal or the reason for having these spaces is simply to re-peasantize the society.

00:36:19.790 --> 00:36:34.450
 Even if people aren't becoming actual full-time small-scale farmers, the awareness of what that looks like and what it means to be a farmer, the awareness of the food system as a whole is just something that needs to be sort of grown from the start.

00:36:34.450 --> 00:36:42.830
 And so it's such a fundamental thing to have any political change, to have people be aware of the system that they're trying to change.

00:36:43.450 --> 00:37:02.840
 That's what I think, like so many of these projects, even if they're not creating farmers, they're creating people with farmer awareness and calling it a farm and having people participate in that way as much as possible to see what the labor really takes also is part of that.

00:37:02.840 --> 00:37:17.940
 I think that, yeah, of course, there's problems with sort of equating, you know, urban farm participation with, you know, being, you know, someone who has a small piece of land in the highlands of Guatemala or something.

00:37:17.940 --> 00:37:28.900
 But at the same time, getting back to our, you know, our conversation about sort of the diversified interests and diversified income streams, that that's something that farmers do a lot of places.

00:37:29.060 --> 00:37:34.450
 A lot of places people cannot survive through purely subsistence agriculture.

00:37:34.690 --> 00:37:39.870
 Most peasants are actually growing partly for their own family's subsistence as well as for local markets.

00:37:39.870 --> 00:37:48.510
 And they're usually growing a variety of crops and they might have off farm work and they might be engaged in different crafts or other forms of income generation.

00:37:48.510 --> 00:37:53.870
 And the same thing holds for us who are trying to create new forms of urban existence.

00:37:53.870 --> 00:38:02.970
 We're trying to create new forms of commons where people can participate and put effort into something that's then rewarding to them in a sort of tangible way.

00:38:03.210 --> 00:38:05.250
 And that's what I think a lot of these projects are.

00:38:05.370 --> 00:38:07.230
 They're urban farm commons.

00:38:07.230 --> 00:38:13.810
 And then we're also trying to figure out our own individual ways of sustaining our livelihoods, right?

00:38:13.890 --> 00:38:28.860
 And so I'm hoping that that's something that we're creating a base for, whether they're, you know, new small businesses that are working on various aspects beyond food, whether they're new collective living enterprises.

00:38:28.860 --> 00:38:36.340
 There's a lot of different things that I think we're doing that are similar in the sense of creating a diversified base for survival.

00:38:36.340 --> 00:38:44.270
 And let's kind of wrap back around to thinking about voting with your fork versus other kinds of participation in a food system.

00:38:44.270 --> 00:39:02.720
 So when you describe what these urban farms are, and I think really creating opportunities for people to participate in their food systems as like active participants, like you would in democracy or other systems like that, it's pretty different than shopping at Whole Foods, for example.

00:39:03.180 --> 00:39:04.460
 How is that different?

00:39:04.460 --> 00:39:13.140
 And maybe you can talk a little bit about Occupy the Farm or that kind of connecting things.

00:39:13.660 --> 00:39:25.040
 I think, I mean, well, I could talk about Occupy the Farm with what I think I would do in relating to my theories of change comment earlier is that, you know, so I was involved in, you know, the energy you were talking about 2005.

00:39:25.420 --> 00:39:31.580
 From 2005 to 2010, there was this explosion of projects in San Francisco dealing with urban ag and food in general.

00:39:32.240 --> 00:39:44.860
 And that was the impetus for me to actually start the San Francisco Urban Agriculture Alliance because there was so much of this energy and I wanted to make sure that it was utilized to its best extent and in a cooperative fashion.

00:39:45.120 --> 00:39:50.980
 You know, you could have multiple nonprofits all trying to do the same thing and then fighting for the same funds and it's the usual situation.

00:39:51.560 --> 00:39:55.240
 So I thought it was beneficial to have people come together on that level.

00:39:55.320 --> 00:40:01.400
 So these were all even themselves possibly collective projects like Alamany Farm that had their own organizational structures.

00:40:01.400 --> 00:40:10.760
 And then they were sort of scaling up into this Urban Agriculture Alliance, which ended up working on policy that wasn't necessarily our initial vision.

00:40:11.540 --> 00:40:17.540
 But the point being that there's various forms of collective action that can make change.

00:40:17.540 --> 00:40:30.860
 So SFUAA ended up being largely about policy because we found opportunities to change policy that was good for urban agriculture in San Francisco and then also on the state level.

00:40:31.280 --> 00:40:50.410
 But then I also was involved in Occupy the Farm, which although it was organized initially in a sort of clandestine fashion because it was an illegal occupation of a piece of land, was really similarly premised on participation drives it and the openness to people being involved and trying to engage people on this site.

00:40:50.410 --> 00:41:00.470
 And in that case, you know, the community of Albany where the site is located had been trying to access the site to turn it into a community farm for 15 years.

00:41:00.970 --> 00:41:16.090
 And so the occupation was what enabled a lot of residents from the area who are excited about that vision to live that vision in the moment, not even having waited for permission or after 15 years of pushing the university to try to acknowledge this.

00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:18.140
 That never worked.

00:41:18.140 --> 00:41:28.050
 And so instead, occupation allowed this experience of, wow, this is what it would look like if this was an educational, agroecological urban farm.

00:41:28.390 --> 00:41:50.430
 And I think that to some degree, all of these forms of collective action are the same thing where when you get to the point where you are seeing something in action, when you're seeing people working together in action, when you're seeing what the farm looks like, when you're seeing that it's possible to make change as communities in a way that as individuals we can't.

00:41:50.430 --> 00:41:54.740
 It just opens up the imagination and inspires people to continue that.

00:41:55.740 --> 00:42:05.420
 Of course, there's going to be times where things go bad, you know, collective projects where there's dysfunction within the group or, you know, political battles where you lose again and again.

00:42:05.420 --> 00:42:08.220
 And I mean, of course, there's nuance to this.

00:42:08.280 --> 00:42:11.920
 It's not all some great thing that always results in what you want.

00:42:12.160 --> 00:42:16.360
 But I think that that's where that's where to me, all of these theories of change can be combined.

00:42:16.360 --> 00:42:36.210
 You can have a worker owned cooperative green enterprise of some sort that is operating on a sort of commercial model, but it's still premised on people working together in a way that is soul fulfilling and actually, you know, socially respectful, mutually respectful.

00:42:36.230 --> 00:42:49.890
 The same way you can have a direct action group that's all about, you know, subverting the private property regime and instilling these new values of agrarian citizenship on public land, like occupy the farm.

00:42:50.110 --> 00:42:55.150
 And the same way you can have a group of people who get together to affect policy that affects us all.

00:42:55.510 --> 00:43:07.190
 You know, you don't need to have only one theory of change as long as that sort of fundamental experience is one of people experiencing their collective power and learning how to use that better and better.

00:43:07.190 --> 00:43:13.090
 Well, so let's finish up by talking about your hopes.

00:43:13.790 --> 00:43:19.340
 And it's a particularly poignant moment as it has been in this city and this region.

00:43:20.000 --> 00:43:31.190
 What do you imagine, you know, for your city and for this region in terms of ongoing food projects and what food sovereignty maybe looks like here over time?

00:43:32.220 --> 00:43:34.550
 That's a tough one.

00:43:35.310 --> 00:43:46.450
 It's a tough one for me because I've out of all of the things that I think about, the one that I've had the least hope for is this city specifically.

00:43:47.090 --> 00:43:59.540
 I don't see much of a future for the image that San Francisco has had as a place for alternatives, for counterculture, for people who don't belong, for ideas that don't belong.

00:43:59.740 --> 00:44:08.340
 I don't know how much longer San Francisco is really going to authentically represent that or if it even does at this moment.

00:44:08.620 --> 00:44:19.180
 I still think that there's an importance to that work being done here because there is this sort of platform that San Francisco still has and its influence around the world.

00:44:19.760 --> 00:44:26.420
 So I do still think it's important, but I don't know if I personally have hope that San Francisco will ever be the same again.

00:44:27.200 --> 00:44:36.320
 That said, I don't think that our belief in the ability that the possibility of change is linked to a single location, right?

00:44:36.320 --> 00:44:43.520
 We are working at systems which go into all spaces and can be affected by all spaces.

00:44:43.840 --> 00:44:51.980
 You know, there's no structure which is located somewhere that you can target and that affects everywhere else but doesn't have a reverse effect, right?

00:44:52.040 --> 00:44:56.160
 We can have effects as individuals and as communities from wherever we are.

00:44:56.160 --> 00:45:07.620
 And so I have a lot of hope in terms of the Bay Area as a whole because there is such a legacy of politicized engagement with the food system.

00:45:08.080 --> 00:45:14.660
 So I think that what's going on right now in Oakland, even though they're dealing with a lot of the same challenges of gentrification,

00:45:14.660 --> 00:45:31.120
 is very inspiring because there's new models being created which are really grounded in what's hopeful to me is anti-capitalism combined with a sort of pragmatic approach to how you can make change organizationally

00:45:31.120 --> 00:45:44.720
 and create new institutions as well as, you know, linking it with these other, you know, they always call axes of oppression, you know, there's class-based oppression, there's race-based oppression, there's gender, there's so many things going on.

00:45:44.720 --> 00:46:00.420
 And I feel like the confluence of factors has made it so that certain projects I would name Planting Justice is one of my favorites because they've been able to figure out a way to incorporate all of these critical components into their work as a food justice organization

00:46:00.420 --> 00:46:15.020
 and really push food justice even work into the food sovereignty direction, meaning really more directly dealing with issues of power rather than just sort of, you know, food security issues, just access issues.

00:46:15.580 --> 00:46:34.590
 And so that's what's inspiring to me is that there's new organizational models and new hope in these hybridized forms where you could be a green entrepreneurship sort of project that still has a component that's really, really about radical critique and working with the most marginalized communities around.

00:46:34.590 --> 00:46:39.150
 So that to me on the urban side is really, really inspiring.

00:46:39.790 --> 00:47:04.020
 And then I think on a global level, the fact that La Via Campesina has had various successes at the international, supranational level of policymaking where the influence of the poorest, most marginalized people of the world, which are, you know, small-scale food producers, their voices are being ever more impossible to ignore.

00:47:04.020 --> 00:47:22.320
 And that their influence has now reversed into the belly of the beast where people like myself and other activists who are working from very consumer sort of perspectives or very urban perspectives, we're seeing the connections between our struggles for food sovereignty and theirs.

00:47:22.840 --> 00:47:51.000
 And I think that there's very much incipient, I don't know what the campaign is exactly, but I know that there's a mindset or an approach or an understanding of a growing internationalism among the food movement, which I think that would be the best thing that we could get to is an acknowledgement that we can't, this whole localization, local foods, all that stuff is great.

00:47:51.000 --> 00:48:06.780
 And one part of the solution, but without a globalized movement, without a movement that's aware of its global connections and implications and who is not working on that level, we're not going to have that really transformative sort of impact that we're looking for.

00:48:06.900 --> 00:48:18.790
 And so that's the thing that I think most hopeful to me is how much more that's coming up, that localism is starting to be questioned because we're starting to say, actually, it's not about just local food systems.

00:48:18.790 --> 00:48:28.670
 It's about how do we change the global capitalist industrial food system and the policy structures that are enabling and supporting and continuing those.

00:48:29.550 --> 00:48:30.550
 So what's next for you, Antonio?

00:48:31.210 --> 00:48:39.270
 Well, I'm going to continue teaching with the Urban Permaculture Institute here in San Francisco, which has been really fun and exciting.

00:48:39.570 --> 00:48:50.130
 And then I'm also starting, hopefully in the new year, a new project with the Permaculture Guild that's an urban carbon farming project funded by the San Francisco Department of the Environment.

00:48:50.830 --> 00:49:00.070
 So that involves basically we're going to be applying compost to a few urban farms and testing to see whether that compost application leads to greater carbon sequestration.

00:49:00.070 --> 00:49:10.090
 And if so, it will leverage further funds in future years from the Department of the Environment to go to urban ag sites to add compost, which seems like a good thing.

00:49:10.750 --> 00:49:27.540
 I'm also working on a couple other potential small projects involving a rooftop urban farm in Oakland, which will be doing seed production and some other writing projects about Occupy the Farm and about different scales of food movement activism, you know, doing the usual.

00:49:28.010 --> 00:49:33.750
 I might even eventually get around to writing a whole suite of songs for an album about my newborn son.

00:49:33.950 --> 00:49:34.650
 We'll see.

